### 4.3 \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\) reconstruction

The identification of a \(\Xi\) or \(\Omega\) through their decay vertices is a straightforward extension of \(V^{0}\) identification. Instead of \(\pi\) or p daughters, one of the daughter tracks is a \(\Lambda\). There are more parameters describing the geometry of the reconstructed decay, those describing the \(\Lambda\) decay vertex and those describing the \(\Xi\) or \(\Omega\) decay vertex. The efficiency is also significantly lower than for the reconstruction of a \(V^{0}\) because three tracks must be reconstructed rather than two and the branching ratios of both the \(\Lambda\) and the \(\Xi\) or \(\Omega\) are relevant.

As with \(V^{0}\) reconstruction, default cuts were applied and these cuts were tightened to reach the desired purity. Most of the cuts applied are exactly analogous to those applied for \(V^{0}\) reconstruction and only cuts unique to \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\) reconstruction will be reviewed here. Table 4.4 lists the default cuts used for \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\) reconstruction.

#### 4.3.1 \(\Xi\) reconstruction

As for \(V^{0}\) reconstruction, the background for the \(\Xi\) is dominated by random combinations of primary tracks. This means that on average the distance of closest approach to the primary vertex of the \(\Xi\) will be lower on average than to the background and the DCA to the primary vertex of the \(\Lambda\) will be higher on average than the background. The DCA of the \(\Lambda\) daughter to the primary vertex and the DCA of the \(\Xi\) candidate are strongly correlated for real \(\Xi\).

