### 6.2 The _Ridge_

The _Ridge_ is an unexpected phenomenon first observed in \(Au+Au\) collisions. In order to understand its origins and to provide a robust test of models for the production of the _Ridge_, the _Ridge_ is studied as a function of as many variables as possible. The collision energy, collision system, system size, and trigger particle type dependence of the _Ridge_ are presented here.

#### Energy and system dependence

Figure 6.10 shows the dependence of the _Ridge_ yield, \(Y_{Ridge}\), on \(N_{part}\) for \(Cu+Cu\) and \(Au+Au\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 62 GeV and \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV. No difference is observed between \(Cu+Cu\) and \(Au+Au\) collisions at either energy. \(Y_{Ridge}\) is larger at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV than \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 62 GeV, as was observed for \(Y_{Jet}\). Figure 6.11 shows that the ratios \(Y_{Ridge}/Y_{Jet}\) are the same for collisions at both \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 62 GeV and\(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV; both the jet-like correlation and the _Ridge_ are about 40% smaller in collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 62 GeV than in collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 200 GeV.

